<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="47149" href="schedule/2015/spring/ENGL/119/47149.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2015" href="schedule/2015.xml">2015</calendarYear><term id="120151" href="schedule/2015/spring.xml">Spring 2015</term><subject id="ENGL" href="schedule/2015/spring/ENGL.xml">English</subject><course id="119" href="schedule/2015/spring/ENGL/119.xml">Literature of Fantasy</course></parents><sectionNumber>E  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Introduction to the rich traditions of fantasy writing in world literature. While the commercial category of fantasy post-Tolkien will often be the focal point, individual instructors may choose to focus on alternate definitions of the genre: literatures of the fantastic, the uncanny, and the weird; fantasy before the Enlightenment and the advent of realism; fantasy for young adult or child readers; and so on.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>CrossListOpen</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2015-01-20Z</startDate><endDate>2015-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>01:00 PM</start><end>01:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MWF    </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>69</roomNumber><buildingName>English Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Sahn" firstName="S">Sahn, S</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>